  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 257 AM MDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nFollowing a chilly August morning where efficient radiational \ncooling allowed high-altitude basins to drop into the 30s and \nlower valleys into the 40s, eastern Idaho remains under the \ninfluence of a broad upper-level trough over the Pacific \nNorthwest. Based on the latest convective-allowing models \n(CAMs), the region will remain precipitation-free through the \ndaytime hours. Afternoon maximum temperatures will generally \nreach the 70s to lower 80s across lower elevations. Moisture and\natmospheric lift will increase by early evening, initiating \nisolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms. This activity \nwill primarily target the eastern half of the forecast area and \nis expected to continue through the overnight hours, potentially\nlingering into early Tuesday morning.\n\nAs Monday night's precipitation clears out, a secondary, \nimpulse embedded within the upper flow is slated to approach \nwestern Idaho later on Tuesday. The trajectory of this impulse \nwill shift the primary axis of atmospheric lift and moisture \nfurther north. Consequently, precipitation probabilities on \nTuesday will be highest across the Central Mountains and \nnorthward toward the Montana Divide and across the upper Snake \nPlain while locations further south and east see more limited \ncoverage. Temperatures will remain steady in the 70s to lower \n80s.\n\nBy mid-to-late week, global ensemble guidance is in good \nagreement locking a broad upper-level cyclone near Vancouver \nIsland, maintaining persistent cyclonic flow across the \nIntermountain West. Current guidance suggests the bulk of the \ndeep atmospheric moisture will remain shunted north and west of \neastern Idaho from Wednesday through Friday, though localized \nmountain showers cannot be ruled out. Tightening pressure \ngradients ahead of incoming Pacific impulses will result in \nseveral breezy afternoon periods mid-to-late week. For now, \nNational Blend of Models (NBM) guidance maintains afternoon \ntemperatures at or a few degrees below seasonal averages through\nthe end of the workweek.\n\nLooking ahead to the holiday weekend, models indicate an upper-\nlevel low situated somewhere across the Pacific Northwest. This\npattern has the potential to bring unsettled weather and cooler\ntemperatures to eastern Idaho for Labor Day. However, confidence\nin the exact evolution of this upper-level pattern remains low\nat this time due to significant ensemble spread. We will\ncontinue to monitor trends as the weekend approaches to refine\nprecipitation chances and timing.",
